{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1793309500000-reviewer-adopt-s1.glm-5.3.findings.md collab/1793309000000-engineer-adopt-fix1.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-30 13:34:20 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-30 20:34:20 +07\n\nAdversarial review of the uncommitted §8.10 Clojure adoption (base 64f2885a). I read the full diff, the spec (docs/design/yang.antlr.md §8.10, lines 3514–4073), and the A1–A5 rulings — note: the rulings file is **not** in this worktree's `collab/` (first untracked artifact is 1793309000000); I read it at `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1793292667828-architect-yang-call-kimi-r3-clojure.kimi-k3.1-pro.findings.md`. All probes below were run read-only against the worktree via `clojure -Sdeps` probe alias; no files edited.\n\n## Findings\n\n**P1 | src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1032-1055, 1146-1155 | User-shadowable names capture the new generated entry/bridge code.** The descriptor entry and caller bridge emit bare `get` (1032-1034, 1148, 1153), `=` (1048, 1147), `assoc` (1037, 1041, 1057), `empty?` (1045) as `:variable` nodes lexically inside whatever user binders enclose the fn site or call site. `check-binder!` (clojure.cljc:204-216) permits binding all four; `with-locals` (clojure.cljc:174-177) rejects only `conj`/`vec`; the keyword-call path guards local `get` (clojure.cljc:1205-1207), proving the hazard model is known. `conj` in `outcome-node` (1023) is covered by the existing guard; the four new names are not. Confirmed by probes: `(def f (let [get 5] (fn [x] x))) (f 1)` → host crash \"Cannot apply non-function\" (was `[:yin.k/ok 1]`); `(let [= not] …)`, `(let [empty? not] …)`, `(let [assoc not] …)` around an fn → \"data primitive refused\"/\"Wrong number of args\"; a `get`-shadowing param corrupts the raise path's condition→outcome conversion (`(fn [get] (g))` with `g` throwing → \"Cannot apply non-function\" instead of `[:yin.k/error {:yin.k/message \"boom\"}]`). The base emits none of these bare (verified by grep of `git show 64f2885a`). Fix: reject local bindings of `get`/`=`/`assoc`/`empty?` at fn/guest-call sites (keyword-call precedent), or emit them through dsg__-bound aliases established once beside the prepended `call/uast` (the data module exports no `get`/`assoc`, so `data/`-qualification is not available).\n\n**P1 | src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1057-1059 + src/cljc/yang/core/call.cljc:33-40 | Ordinary map writes forge callables; the data-map boundary fixture is missing.** Descriptors are plain immutable maps built by guest `assoc`, and `yang.call/eligible?` is purely structural (any map with non-nil `:yang.call/entry`). Probe: `(let [h (assoc {} :yang.call/entry (get f :yang.call/entry))] (h 21))` → `[:yin.k/ok 42]` — an ordinary `assoc` mints a working callable. The ruling's Additional obligations state \"A source map containing `:yang.call/entry` remains data unless constructed by the pinned callable constructor/projection. Ordinary map writes must not overwrite or forge the runtime entry\", and the 8.10.11 acceptance gate requires exercising \"source data with an entry-spelled property remains data\" (yang.antlr.md:4029-4031). No negative fixture exists; `test-common-call-hand-constructed-descriptor` (test/yang/clojure_test.clj:1183-1191) enshrines the opposite admission. Mitigating context: the orchestrator prompt (collab/1793338209376-…prompt.md item 5) explicitly directed the hand-constructed fixture \"as the spec's own illustration shows\". This needs architect arbitration: either seal/admit via a pinned constructor, or record the structural admission as this profile's defined projection (yang.antlr.md:3590-3592 gives cover) and add the boundary fixtures (non-callable entry → stays data; forged/assoc'd descriptor → refused).\n\n**P2 | src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1163-1169 | A2's \"a missing entry means not callable\" is not enforced for source applications.** The routing is `(if (yang.call/eligible? callee) common-call direct-call)` — a value-structural exemption (\"anything without an entry applies directly\"), not dsg__/private-scoped. `:yang.call/not-callable` is unreachable from compiled source calls; an entry-less foreign callable (e.g. a pre-migration py/js cell, which is a map) surfaces as a raw VM defect — probe: `(let [x 5] (x 1))` → host \"Cannot apply non-function\" — rather than the qualified condition. The reachable closure cases (adapters, macro lambdas, continuations) do work, and the orchestrator prompt item 2 sanctioned \"plain non-descriptor closures (internal, private, pre-adopted program values) apply directly as today\", which is broader than the architect's \"internal compiler-generated calls may remain private\". Fix: record the deviation with the architect, or scope the direct branch (e.g. compiler-generated callees only) so missing-entry foreign values get the protocol condition.\n\n**P2 | test/yang/clojure_test.clj:1355-1384 | Tail-chain stack growth: a documented invariant was dropped, not preserved.** The base asserted `d100 = d1000` (\"depth constant in the iteration count\") and the base comment (clojure.cljc:618-621, rewritten in this diff) guaranteed \"the semantic VM's return stack stays constant through tail chains\". The renamed `test-outcome-common-call-depth` now *asserts* linear growth — 9 frames per source activation (`(= (- d1000 d100) (* 9 (- d100 d0)))`) — and deleted the constant-depth assertion for both lanes. `loop`/`recur` chains remain constant (`test-continuation-depth-gates`, clojure_test.clj:1088). No hard VM depth limit exists (3000-deep named tail recursion completes), but deep named/mutual recursion now consumes ~9 frames/activation, and A2's obligation list includes \"tail behavior\" (fast-path preservation; no fast path exists here). Fix: acceptable only as a recorded architect tradeoff with a follow-up fast path; otherwise the entry/bridge needs a constant-stack design for tail calls.\n\n**P3 | src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1165-1169 | Silent bad-value fallback when the captured continuation is not callable.** `(if (data/callable? escape) …common/direct… escape)` returns the continuation itself as the call's value instead of refusing. Unreachable on both current lanes (walker and semantic both evaluate `%capture` to a callable), but the branch should refuse, not yield a continuation as a value.\n\n**P3 | src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1035-1042, 1052-1055 | Named-argument binding errors ride `:yang.call/thrown`.** The nonempty-named rejection produces a slice-5 error map raised as a `:yang.call/thrown` condition, indistinguishable at the caller from a guest throw; 8.10.7 (yang.antlr.md:3852-3858) provides qualified reasons (e.g. `:yang.call/unsupported-binding`) for callee binding failures. Data is preserved either way (probes confirmed message/data/named round-trip), so informational.\n\n## Verified conformant (with evidence)\n\n- **A1**: descriptors at every source fn site — defn/defn- (`compile-av-fn`, clojure.cljc:1078-1080), anonymous fn with source meta (1668), fn-literal def values, multi-arity, and guest-library defns (probes: `map`/`partial`/`comp`/`reduce` all receive and invoke descriptors, `[:yin.k/ok (3 4 5)]` etc.); entry is a NEW unary closure (`u/lam [invocation]`) over the private av body, never the body itself; `:clj/type :function` asserted by test (clojure_test.clj:1168-1175); defmacro lambdas correctly excluded (clojure.cljc:454-475, plain `compile-lambda`).\n- **A3**: every caller passes `(u/lit [])` (clojure.cljc:1161, the only `yang.call/invoke` emission); entry rejects nonempty named before the body with the message/data fixture (clojure_test.clj:1168-1226); `:yang.call/receiver` never set.\n- **A4**: entry ok→value, error→`raise(yang.call/thrown data)` (1043-1055); the caller's raise closure escapes via the captured continuation with a converted outcome and can never return a condition as a successful value (1146-1157); protocol failures keep the complete condition (else-branch, 1155); arity/anonymous error shapes survive the bridge intact (probes: `{:yin.vm.data/reason :arity, :yin.k/fn f}` and `:yin.k/kind :anonymous` with source position); condition-shaped return maps stay successful values (test 1176-1180).\n- **A5**: adapters stay plain closures (1111-1122), dsg__ temps stay closures, the slice-8 kernel is untouched (the oracle test now reaches *into* the published entry for the captured unparser rather than the closure — latte_oracle_test.clj:1291-1294).\n- **Hang fix**: `discard-result` (1855-1856, 1877, 1890, 1900) only alters the never-referenced `_`/`%_` sequencing binding — def values live in the yin/def store, the final form's value is untouched, and `call/uast` is *prepended* (the `cond->>` threads the program as `u/then`'s second arg, clojure.cljc:1904-1905), so the program's value remains the last form's outcome (confirmed by the green outcome tests); non-outcome mode is unchanged. The engineer's fix1 log confirms the old sequencing exhausts the 100,001-visit budget and the new bounded assertions (latte_oracle_test.clj:1238-1250) pin it.\n- **Regressions green together (JVM)**: `yang.clojure-test` 81/690, `yang.latte-oracle-test` 21/971, `yang.core.call-test` 4/13, `yang.python.antlr.call-entry-test` 1/4, `yin.vm.macro-test` 38/497, `yang.clojure.stream-eval-test` 13/309, `yang.frontend-test` + `yang.cross-lang-test` green — 0 failures everywhere.\n\nNo P0s. The two P1s are the gate: the shadow-capture hole is an outright soundness bug in the new generated code, and the forgery/boundary question needs the architect's ruling before commit.\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-30 13:00:34 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-30 20:00:34 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\nFixed exponential validation caused by discarded top-level descriptors being retained in subsequent closure environments. Sequencing now clears those discarded results.\n\nAdded bounded validation and execution checks around the oracle checkpoint round-trip. Confirmed the regression stops the old sequencing at 100,001 validation visits.\n\n- Oracle: 21 tests, 971 assertions; zero failures/errors.\n- Requested four namespaces: 123 tests, 1,200 assertions; zero failures/errors.\n- Kondo: zero errors, two existing warnings.\n- `git diff --check`: clean.\n\nChanges remain uncommitted.","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
