{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_47","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -i 'deviation|deviations|ledger' collab/*.md collab/1793338209376-engineer-clojure-adoption-s1.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log collab/1793338209376-engineer-clojure-adoption-s1.kimi-k3.1-pro.stdout.log | tail -35\"","aggregated_output":"collab/1793309500000-reviewer-adopt-s1.glm-5.3.findings.md:12:**P2 | src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1163-1169 | A2's \"a missing entry means not callable\" is not enforced for source applications.** The routing is `(if (yang.call/eligible? callee) common-call direct-call)` — a value-structural exemption (\"anything without an entry applies directly\"), not dsg__/private-scoped. `:yang.call/not-callable` is unreachable from compiled source calls; an entry-less foreign callable (e.g. a pre-migration py/js cell, which is a map) surfaces as a raw VM defect — probe: `(let [x 5] (x 1))` → host \"Cannot apply non-function\" — rather than the qualified condition. The reachable closure cases (adapters, macro lambdas, continuations) do work, and the orchestrator prompt item 2 sanctioned \"plain non-descriptor closures (internal, private, pre-adopted program values) apply directly as today\", which is broader than the architect's \"internal compiler-generated calls may remain private\". Fix: record the deviation with the architect, or scope the direct branch (e.g. compiler-generated callees only) so missing-entry foreign values get the protocol condition.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
