 of master — verified, so the branch carries the approved spec.\n- Adoption branch/worktree: `yang-common-call` / `datomworld-yang-common-call`, cut\n  after the slice-9 close-out. Slice-9's BLOCKED gate (branch yang-slice9, 7dc18478)\n  stays preserved on its own branch; the adoption rebase does not absorb it.\n\n## Dependencies and sequencing\n\n1. Coordinating owner pins the shared runtime API + profile (blocking for Clojure wiring).\n2. Architect settles the Clojure-specific rulings (below) — dispatchable in parallel with 1.\n3. Bounded engineering rounds on this branch against the pinned runtime.\n4. Cross-language cases (TS/PHP/Python/Clojure) only after every frontend revision is pinned.\n\n## Architect decisions requested (Clojure-specific, before coding)\n\nA1. Entry publication for yang.clojure callables: function values in this dialect are\n    plain closures defined via yin/def (no cell wrapper today). Ruling requested: does a\n    conforming Clojure callable become an immutable descriptor map {:yang.call/entry f\n    :clj/type :function ...} returned by a constructor, with defn sites constructing it —\n    or does the existing direct-closure callable remain a second admitted kind with the\n    descriptor only at the cross-language boundary?\nA2. Source-call-site lowering: does every source application route through the common\n    projection (checking for a descriptor) with fast-path plain-closure application, or\n    do only cross-language-annotated sites route through it?\nA3. Invocation record: `:yang.call/positional` is a vector — Clojure application is\n    already vector-based, so no shape change; ruling requested on `:yang.call/named`\n    (Clojure has no named args — absent key vs empty vector).\nA4. Outcome mapping: the slice-5 outcome model ([yin.k/error data]) vs §8.10.7's\n    raise-exit — ruling requested: a guest Clojure throw lowers to an error outcome\n    internally; at a cross-language boundary the outcome converts to the raise-exit\n    condition. Confirm this two-regime mapping is conformant (no leak of error tuples\n    as values; no new VM case).\nA5. Internal helpers (the compiler's dsg__ temps, the guest library's private closures,\n    the slice-8 kernel context) stay non-source callables — confirm they are NOT rewritten\n    as conforming descriptors (§8.10.2's last paragraph).\nzsh:1: no matches found: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/*ledger*\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
